<!-- 4 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-class">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">04</span><h2>Classification &amp; Why It Matters</h2></div>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="vcard v-teal">
      <span class="vtag">Coarse-grained (sand, gravel)</span>
      <h4>Behaviour set by grain size and packing</h4>
      <p>Drains fast, so loading is effectively <strong>drained</strong> &mdash; pore pressures dissipate as quickly as you apply load. Strength is almost pure friction (c' &asymp; 0). Settlement is immediate and essentially over by the end of construction.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb">
      <span class="vtag">Fine-grained (silt, clay)</span>
      <h4>Behaviour set by mineralogy and water</h4>
      <p>Drains very slowly, so short-term loading is <strong>undrained</strong>. Has real cohesion. Settlement continues for years. Characterised by Atterberg limits &mdash; liquid limit LL, plastic limit PL, plasticity index PI = LL &minus; PL.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>Core Idea</span>
    <p>Classification is not paperwork &mdash; it decides <strong>which analysis you are allowed to run</strong>. Sand &rarr; drained, effective stress, c' = 0. Clay short-term &rarr; undrained, &phi; = 0, use c<sub>u</sub>. Clay long-term &rarr; drained, use c' and &phi;'. Choosing the wrong one is the most common conceptual error in geotechnical design.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 5 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-eff">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">05</span><h2>The Effective Stress Principle</h2></div>

  <p class="lead">If you remember one equation from CEE 240, this is it. Terzaghi's effective stress principle is the single most important idea in all of soil mechanics.</p>

  <div class="vcard v-vio" style="margin-bottom:1rem">
    <span class="vtag">Terzaghi, 1925</span>
    <div class="eq">\[ \sigma = \sigma' + u \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad \sigma' = \sigma - u \]</div>
  </div>

  <div class="analogy">
    <span class="an-icon">&#127946;</span>
    <div>
      <strong>Stand in a swimming pool.</strong> Your weight has not changed, but you press on the floor far more gently &mdash; the water is carrying part of you. Soil grains behave identically: the pore water holds part of the load, and only the remainder, pressed grain against grain, generates friction. Water has no shear strength, so <strong>every kilopascal carried by water is a kilopascal that produces no strength.</strong>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="vcard v-teal"><span class="vtag">Total stress &sigma;</span><p>Weight of everything above, soil and water together. What a pressure cell would read.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-blu"><span class="vtag">Pore pressure u</span><p>Pressure in the water in the voids. Hydrostatic below a static water table: u = &gamma;<sub>w</sub>z<sub>w</sub>.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb"><span class="vtag">Effective stress &sigma;'</span><p>The grain-to-grain contact stress. <strong>Controls strength, stiffness and volume change &mdash; nothing else does.</strong></p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>This one principle explains the whole course.</strong> Rain raises u, drops &sigma;', and slopes fail. Loading clay raises u first and &sigma;' only slowly &mdash; that <em>is</em> consolidation. A high water table raises u under a footing and halves its bearing capacity. Every dramatic number in the following chapters is this subtraction doing its work.</div>
  </div>
</section>

