<!-- 7 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-perm">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">07</span><h2>Permeability &amp; Seepage</h2></div>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="vcard v-vio">
      <span class="vtag">Darcy's law</span>
      <div class="eq">\[ v = ki, \qquad i = \frac&#123;\Delta h&#125;&#123;L&#125;, \qquad q = kiA \]</div>
      <p class="small">k spans an extraordinary range: gravel ~10&#8315;&sup1; m/s down to clay ~10&#8315;&#185;&#8304; m/s &mdash; nine orders of magnitude.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-blu">
      <span class="vtag">Why that range matters</span>
      <p>It is the entire reason "drained" and "undrained" are different analyses. Load sand and the water leaves before you finish; load clay and the water is still leaving a decade later. Permeability, not strength, decides which case you are in &mdash; and it also sets c<sub>v</sub>, the rate of consolidation.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 8 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-shear">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">08</span><h2>Shear Strength &mdash; Mohr&ndash;Coulomb</h2></div>

  <p>Soil does not fail in tension or compression. It fails in <strong>shear</strong> &mdash; one block sliding past another. Everything in this course that "fails" is a shear failure on some surface.</p>

  <div class="vcard v-vio" style="margin-bottom:1rem">
    <span class="vtag">The failure criterion</span>
    <div class="eq">\[ \tau_f = c' + \sigma'\tan\phi' \]</div>
    <p class="small">Note it is &sigma;', not &sigma;. Strength depends on effective stress &mdash; this is where Section 5 meets Section 8.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="vcard v-teal"><span class="vtag">Cohesion c'</span><p>Strength at zero confinement &mdash; grains genuinely sticking together. Real in clay, essentially zero in clean sand.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb"><span class="vtag">Friction angle &phi;'</span><p>How much extra strength each unit of confinement buys. Interlocking and sliding between grains. Dominant in sand.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-rust"><span class="vtag">Undrained strength c<sub>u</sub></span><p>For clay loaded fast: use &phi; = 0 and &tau;<sub>f</sub> = c<sub>u</sub>. Not a different material &mdash; a different <em>drainage condition</em>.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>Core Idea</span>
    <p>Two soils can have identical &tau;<sub>f</sub> today for completely different reasons &mdash; one from cohesion, one from friction &mdash; and behave nothing alike when conditions change. The Chattogram case study in the slope chapter is exactly this: the site with the <em>highest</em> cohesion failed worst, because it had almost no friction to fall back on when rain softened it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 9 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-stress">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">09</span><h2>Stress Increase with Depth</h2></div>

  <p>A footing applies pressure at the surface, but the layer that matters may be 5 m down. How much of that pressure reaches it?</p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="vcard v-teal">
      <span class="vtag">The 2:1 approximation</span>
      <div class="eq">\[ \Delta\sigma = \frac&#123;qBL&#125;&#123;(B+z)(L+z)&#125; \]</div>
      <p class="small">Assume the load spreads outward at 2 vertical to 1 horizontal. Crude, quick, and good enough for most settlement estimates.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-vio">
      <span class="vtag">Boussinesq</span>
      <p>The rigorous elastic solution for a point or distributed load on a semi-infinite elastic half-space, usually applied through influence factors and charts. More accurate, more work.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>This is what sets borehole depth.</strong> The rule from the soil investigation chapter &mdash; bore until &Delta;&sigma; &le; 0.1q &mdash; is just this equation solved for z. It is also how you get the &Delta;&sigma;' that goes into the settlement calculation.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 10 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-map">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">10</span><h2>Where Each Idea Reappears</h2></div>

  <div class="qref">
    <div><span>&gamma;' = &gamma;<sub>sat</sub> &minus; &gamma;<sub>w</sub></span><span>slope stability, bearing capacity</span></div>
    <div><span>&sigma;' = &sigma; &minus; u</span><span>every single chapter</span></div>
    <div><span>&tau;<sub>f</sub> = c' + &sigma;'tan&phi;'</span><span>earth pressure, slopes, bearing capacity</span></div>
    <div><span>Void ratio e</span><span>settlement (C<sub>c</sub>, C<sub>s</sub>)</span></div>
    <div><span>Permeability k</span><span>time rate of consolidation c<sub>v</sub></span></div>
    <div><span>Drained vs. undrained</span><span>which strength parameters are legal</span></div>
    <div><span>&Delta;&sigma; with depth</span><span>settlement, borehole depth</span></div>
    <div><span>Atterberg limits</span><span>classification, &alpha; for piles</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>Core Idea</span>
    <p>If a later chapter stops making sense, the problem is almost always one of these eight rows &mdash; most often the difference between &sigma; and &sigma;', or between drained and undrained. Come back here first.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 11 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-examples">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">11</span><h2>Worked Examples</h2></div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 1</span><h4>Phase Relations from Three Numbers</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> A soil has G<sub>s</sub> = 2.68, water content w = 18%, void ratio e = 0.72. Find porosity, degree of saturation, dry unit weight, moist unit weight, saturated unit weight and submerged unit weight.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Porosity:</strong> \( n = \dfrac&#123;e&#125;&#123;1+e&#125; = \dfrac&#123;0.72&#125;&#123;1.72&#125; = 0.419 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Saturation:</strong> \( S = \dfrac&#123;G_s w&#125;&#123;e&#125; = \dfrac&#123;2.68(0.18)&#125;&#123;0.72&#125; = 0.670 = 67\% \) &mdash; partially saturated</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Dry unit weight:</strong> \( \gamma_d = \dfrac&#123;G_s\gamma_w&#125;&#123;1+e&#125; = \dfrac&#123;2.68(9.81)&#125;&#123;1.72&#125; = 15.29\text&#123;kN/m&#125;^3 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Moist:</strong> \( \gamma = \gamma_d(1+w) = 15.29(1.18) = 18.04\text&#123;kN/m&#125;^3 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>5</b><div><strong>Saturated:</strong> \( \gamma_&#123;sat&#125; = \dfrac&#123;(G_s+e)\gamma_w&#125;&#123;1+e&#125; = \dfrac&#123;(2.68+0.72)(9.81)&#125;&#123;1.72&#125; = 19.39\text&#123;kN/m&#125;^3 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>6</b><div><strong>Submerged:</strong> \( \gamma' = 19.39 - 9.81 = 9.58\text&#123;kN/m&#125;^3 \)</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> n = 0.419, S = 67%, &gamma;<sub>d</sub> = 15.29, &gamma; = 18.04, &gamma;<sub>sat</sub> = 19.39, &gamma;' = 9.58 kN/m&sup3;. Note &gamma;' is <strong>half</strong> of &gamma;<sub>sat</sub> &mdash; remember that ratio; it drives most of this course.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 2</span><h4>Effective Stress Profile</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> 3 m of moist sand (&gamma; = 17 kN/m&sup3;) sits over 5 m of saturated sand (&gamma;<sub>sat</sub> = 20 kN/m&sup3;). The water table is at 3 m depth. Find &sigma;, u and &sigma;' at 3 m and at 8 m.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>At 3 m</strong> (just at the water table): \( \sigma = 17(3) = 51.0\text&#123;kPa&#125; \), \( u = 0 \), so \( \sigma' = 51.0\text&#123;kPa&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>At 8 m, total stress:</strong> \( \sigma = 51.0 + 20(5) = 151.0\text&#123;kPa&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Pore pressure:</strong> \( u = \gamma_w z_w = 9.81(5) = 49.1\text&#123;kPa&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Effective stress:</strong> \( \sigma' = 151.0 - 49.1 = 101.9\text&#123;kPa&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>5</b><div><strong>Sanity check</strong> using &gamma;' directly: \( \sigma' = 51.0 + (20-9.81)(5) = 51.0 + 51.0 = 102.0\text&#123;kPa&#125; \) &check; &mdash; the two routes must agree.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> At 8 m, &sigma; = 151.0 kPa but &sigma;' = 101.9 kPa. Water is carrying a third of the load. Raise the water table to the surface and &sigma;' drops further still &mdash; try it in the calculator above.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 12 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-quiz">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">12</span><h2>Quick Reference &amp; Quick Check</h2></div>

  <div class="qref">
    <div><span>Effective stress</span><span>&sigma;' = &sigma; &minus; u</span></div>
    <div><span>Porosity</span><span>n = e/(1+e)</span></div>
    <div><span>Saturation</span><span>S = G<sub>s</sub>w/e</span></div>
    <div><span>Dry unit weight</span><span>&gamma;<sub>d</sub> = G<sub>s</sub>&gamma;<sub>w</sub>/(1+e)</span></div>
    <div><span>Saturated unit weight</span><span>&gamma;<sub>sat</sub> = (G<sub>s</sub>+e)&gamma;<sub>w</sub>/(1+e)</span></div>
    <div><span>Submerged unit weight</span><span>&gamma;' = &gamma;<sub>sat</sub> &minus; &gamma;<sub>w</sub> &asymp; &frac12;&gamma;<sub>sat</sub></span></div>
    <div><span>Shear strength</span><span>&tau;<sub>f</sub> = c' + &sigma;'tan&phi;'</span></div>
    <div><span>Darcy</span><span>v = ki, q = kiA</span></div>
    <div><span>2:1 stress spread</span><span>&Delta;&sigma; = qBL/[(B+z)(L+z)]</span></div>
    <div><span>&gamma;<sub>w</sub></span><span>9.81 kN/m&sup3;</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz" id="quiz">
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>1. The water table rises to the ground surface. What happens to total stress &sigma; and effective stress &sigma;' at 5 m depth?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Both increase</button>
      <button data-v="1">&sigma; increases slightly (soil now saturated) while &sigma;' decreases — u rises faster</button>
      <button data-v="2">Both are unchanged; water does not affect stress</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="2">
      <p>2. Why does a clay need a different analysis in the short term than in the long term?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Clay gets stronger with age</button>
      <button data-v="1">The load changes over time</button>
      <button data-v="2">Clay drains so slowly that short-term loading is undrained (&phi; = 0, c<sub>u</sub>) while long-term is drained (c', &phi;')</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="0">
      <p>3. Two soils both have &tau;<sub>f</sub> = 40 kPa at the current stress. Soil A is all cohesion, soil B is all friction. Rain saturates both. Which is in more trouble?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Soil A — its cohesion can be softened by water, and it has no friction to fall back on</button>
      <button data-v="1">Soil B — friction disappears entirely when wet</button>
      <button data-v="2">Neither; they have the same strength so they behave identically</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>4. Why is submerged unit weight roughly half the saturated value?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Because half the soil dissolves</button>
      <button data-v="1">&gamma;' = &gamma;<sub>sat</sub> − &gamma;<sub>w</sub>, and &gamma;<sub>w</sub> = 9.81 is close to half a typical &gamma;<sub>sat</sub> ≈ 20</button>
      <button data-v="2">Because porosity is always 50%</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="quiz-score" class="quiz-score"></div>
</section>

